---
import BaseLayout from "../../../shared/components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { MAIN_SITE_URL, DATA_SITE_URL } from "../../../shared/config/config";

const OPERATOR_INFO = "{OPERATOR-INFO}";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  name: "About the Project",
  url: MAIN_SITE_URL + "/about/",
  description: "About the Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform: a structured knowledge and experience platform for people seeking to understand and recover from problematic pornography use.",
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", "name": "Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform", "url": "https://selfcontrolatlas.com" },
};
---
<BaseLayout
  siteTitle="Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform"
  siteUrl={MAIN_SITE_URL}
  title="About the Project"
  description="About the Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform: a structured knowledge and experience platform for people seeking to understand and recover from problematic pornography use."
  canonical="/about/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "About", url: "/about/" }]}
>
  <h1>About the Project</h1>
  <p>SelfControlAtlas is a structured knowledge and experience platform for people who
  want to understand and recover from problematic pornography use (PPU) and related
  compulsive sexual behaviors. This page explains what the project is, who makes it, how
  it is governed, and — just as importantly — what it does not claim to be.</p>

  <h2>What this project is</h2>
  <p>The project is built around four linked assets, each with a distinct role:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Real recovery experiences</strong> — de-identified and structured into comparable records.</li>
    <li><strong>Community patterns</strong> — recurring observations drawn across many experiences, not anecdotes presented as fact.</li>
    <li><strong>Scientific evidence</strong> — summarized independently from published research, kept strictly separate from community data.</li>
    <li><strong>A knowledge graph</strong> — connecting experiences, triggers, strategies, questions, and research so readers can trace how a claim is supported.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The purpose is to make information about PPU and recovery more organized, more
  transparent, and easier to verify than scattered forum posts. Instead of reading dozens
  of disconnected threads, a reader can find a structured account, see which patterns
  recur, and check what the evidence actually says.</p>

  <h3>Why this matters</h3>
  <p>Problematic pornography use is widely discussed online but poorly organized. Two
  problems are common: personal anecdotes get treated as scientific fact, and scientific
  findings get buried or misrepresented. This project addresses both by separating the
  layers explicitly — you can always tell whether you are reading one person's experience,
  an aggregate pattern, or a research summary.</p>

  <h2>What this project is not</h2>
  <p>The project is deliberately clear about its limits:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Nothing here is a diagnosis, treatment plan, or substitute for a qualified professional. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a crisis service.</strong> Messages are not monitored in real time. If you are in crisis, see the <a href="/crisis-resources/">Crisis Resources</a> page.</li>
    <li><strong>Not therapy or coaching.</strong> The site explains evidence; it does not treat, coach, or counsel.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a commercial product.</strong> There is no advertising, no sponsors, no affiliate links, and nothing for sale. See the <a href="/advertising-disclosure/">Advertising Disclosure</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who makes this</h2>
  <p>This project is maintained by:</p>
  <p><strong>{OPERATOR_INFO}</strong></p>
  <p>The operator is responsible for the day-to-day maintenance of the site, the
  de-identification and structuring of community-derived content, and the editorial
  standards described in the <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>.</p>

  <h3>No fabricated experts</h3>
  <p>This site does not claim authority it does not have. Specifically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Content is <strong>not authored or reviewed by named medical or mental-health professionals</strong>, and we do not claim that it is.</li>
    <li>We do not invent expert credentials, author bios, or advisory boards.</li>
    <li>We do not fabricate statistics, studies, or sources.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This honesty is deliberate and central to the project. A recovery resource should
  tell you exactly what it is and what it is not, so you can judge its content
  appropriately.</p>

  <h3>Editorial responsibility</h3>
  <p>Data processing — de-identification, structuring, and editorial rewriting — and
  evidence synthesis are performed by the project's editors, following the published
  <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>.
  Editors work from the evidence tiers described in the methodology; they do not invent
  sources, and they label uncertainty when the evidence is weak.</p>

  <h3>Corrections</h3>
  <p>Factual errors are corrected when identified. The current version of any page is
  authoritative. If you find an error, see the <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> page for how
  to report it.</p>

  <h2>How the content is structured</h2>
  <p>The site uses a four-layer content system, described fully in the
  <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Experiences</strong> — de-identified first-person recovery accounts.</li>
    <li><strong>Patterns</strong> — recurring observations aggregated across experiences.</li>
    <li><strong>Problems</strong> — common questions answered with reference to the evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence</strong> — research summaries tied to published sources and evidence tiers.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Every layer is labeled, and the de-identification rules are published so readers can
  see exactly what is removed before anything is published.</p>

  <h3>What the knowledge graph does</h3>
  <p>The knowledge graph links entities across the site — a trigger page connects to the
  strategies that address it, a problem page connects to the evidence that bears on it, and
  an experience connects to the patterns it illustrates. This lets readers move from "what
  is this?" to "what does the evidence say?" to "what have others experienced?" without
  losing track of which is which.</p>

  <h2>How the site handles your information</h2>
  <p>This site is static and deliberately minimal about data:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No accounts and no sign-ups.</li>
    <li>No collection of names, email addresses, or other personally identifying information.</li>
    <li>No analytics or tracking scripts.</li>
    <li>No cookies.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The only browser-side persistence is the optional <a href="/tools/trigger-journal/">Trigger Journal</a>
  tool, which stores entries in <strong>your own browser's local storage</strong> — data that
  never leaves your device. Full details are in the <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a>
  and <a href="/cookie-policy/">Cookie Policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>Intended audience</h2>
  <p>This site discusses adult topics, including pornography and compulsive sexual
  behavior. It is intended for adults. There is no adult media on the site itself, but the
  subject matter is mature. If you are under 18, this content is not intended for you.</p>

  <h2>Scope and limitations</h2>
  <p>We aim for accuracy and label uncertainty honestly, but information may be incomplete
  or outdated. Research summaries reflect the sources available at the time of writing and
  may be superseded by newer studies. Community-reported content reflects individual
  experiences, not established facts. No page on this site should be read as the final word
  on your situation.</p>

  <h2>Governing principles</h2>
  <p>Three principles shape everything here:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Transparency</strong> — you can see how content is made, labeled, and corrected.</li>
    <li><strong>Honesty about limits</strong> — the site distinguishes evidence from experience and never inflates either.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety</strong> — crisis resources and professional-help signposts are prioritized over any recovery claim.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How to use this site responsibly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read the <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> to understand what kind of content you are reading.</li>
    <li>Distinguish experiences from evidence — the site labels them separately.</li>
    <li>For personal concerns, consult a qualified professional rather than relying on the site alone.</li>
    <li>If you are in crisis, use the <a href="/crisis-resources/">Crisis Resources</a> page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related pages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> — how content is produced and labeled.</li>
    <li><a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a> — production, de-identification, and correction rules.</li>
    <li><a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> — the full medical and psychological disclaimer.</li>
    <li><a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a> — how your information is handled.</li>
    <li><a href="/contact/">Contact</a> — corrections, questions, and how to reach the operator.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><em>Last updated: 2026-10-23.</em></p>
</BaseLayout>
